    <div class="essay-container">
        <h1 class="title">The Weight of Not Trying</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">A Man, His Land, and the Space Between Preservation and Creation</p>
        
        <div class="essay-body">
            <p class="no-indent">I am fifty-six years old, standing at the edge of a decision that carries the weight of three generations. Behind me: three hundred acres reduced to fourteen. Before me: a choice between preservation and transformation. And within me: a question that has kept me awake more nights than I care to count.</p>

            <p>What am I most afraid of? Not failure. Not loss. Not even the judgment of others. What terrifies me most is the possibility that I will die having never tried—that when I take my last breath, the heaviest regret will not be what I attempted and failed at, but what I never had the courage to begin.</p>

            <p>I practice dying every day. Not morbidly, but as a contemplative discipline—waking each morning and asking myself: if today were my last day, would I be content with how things stand? It is a practice that should bring peace, but lately it has brought only clarity, and clarity can be uncomfortable. The answer has been no. Not because my life is without meaning, but because I see what could be and know I have not yet reached for it.</p>

            <div class="section-break">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

            <p class="no-indent">Ten years ago, I did something that seemed insane to many: I laid myself off. Not fired, not downsized—I walked away from the employment system entirely. I chose to live outside the rat race, to build a life grounded in abundance rather than scarcity, in sovereignty rather than dependence. While friends climbed corporate ladders, I tended land and consciousness with equal devotion.</p>

            <p>Now, a decade later, the future I sensed then is catching up with the present. The layoffs, the system fragility, the cultural breakdown—they are no longer distant possibilities but current realities. I was not delusional ten years ago, and I am not delusional now. I am simply seeing the patterns before they become obvious to everyone else.</p>

            <p>This is both my gift and my burden: I sense what is coming. But sensing is not the same as knowing what to do about it.</p>

            <div class="section-break">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

            <p class="no-indent">My ancestors had three hundred acres. They survived by selling "one or two acres as needed to get by." Crisis would come—drought, debt, death—and they would liquidate a piece of their inheritance. Then another crisis. Another sale. Three hundred became one hundred. One hundred became fifty. Fifty became fourteen.</p>

            <p>I am the third generation holding what remains. And I carry the fear that I am simply continuing their pattern—that my son will inherit nine acres, then five, then two, then nothing. That the land will slip through our fingers the way water slips through cupped hands, no matter how tightly we try to hold it.</p>

            <p>But there is a difference, I tell myself. My ancestors sold reactively, during crisis, to survive. I am considering selling proactively, from a position of strength, to transform. They consumed the capital. I would invest it. They preserved what was dying. I want to create what could live.</p>

            <p>Is this rationalization? Or is this the critical distinction between erosion and regeneration? I do not know for certain. And uncertainty is where I have lived for months now.</p>

            <div class="pullquote">
                "Not even trying sits heavier than the possibility of failure."
            </div>

            <p class="no-indent">There is a buyer. He has waited a year. He lives one kilometer away. He wants five of my fourteen acres for sixty-six lakhs per acre—₹3.3 crores total. It is real money. Known money. Certain money.</p>

            <p>I counter-offered at seventy lakhs per acre. He is maxed out at sixty-six. Meanwhile, rumors circulate that displaced landowners from a highway acquisition will be seeking farmland in early 2026, willing to pay one crore per acre. That would be ₹5 crores instead of ₹3.3 crores. But these are rumors, not facts. Wishful projections, not documented realities.</p>

            <p>For months, I have analyzed this decision from every angle. I built tools—progressive web applications—to model the scenarios across timeframes, to run the numbers through my JAR money management system, to project returns and risks and outcomes. I have done the homework. I have run the calculations. I have researched until there is nothing left to research.</p>

            <p>And still, I have not acted.</p>

            <div class="section-break">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

            <p class="no-indent">People assume I am overthinking. But I am not. I have legitimate constraints: the price threshold is not yet met, I distrust the banking system where the capital would sit, I want a buyer whose values align with mine, I want the land to be used well. These are not overthinking—these are careful consideration.</p>

            <p>But there is something deeper. Something I have been reluctant to admit even to myself.</p>

            <p>I am afraid of my own distraction element.</p>

            <p>In 2021, I experienced a health event that changed how my brain works—how I form memories, how I visualize, how I process information. I have studied neuroscience to understand myself. I have built systems to compensate. I have developed techniques for retention and organization. I have adapted brilliantly, if I may say so.</p>

            <p>But I also know myself well enough to see my pattern: deep research that does not always translate into execution. Multiple simultaneous interests—agritech, cybersecurity, consciousness studies, philosophy—that fragment my focus. A mind that explores widely but sometimes struggles to commit deeply.</p>

            <p>So when I imagine selling the five acres and using ₹3.3 crores to fund agricultural transformation experiments—mushroom farming, vertical crops, sustainable alternatives to the dying paddy-wheat system—I see both the vision and the shadow. I see the possibility of creating something generationally sustainable for my son. And I see the possibility that my own distractibility will waste the opportunity.</p>

            <p>This is the fear that keeps me frozen: not that the experiments will fail, but that I will fail to execute them with the focus they require. That I will have sold ancestral land, taken on the responsibility of innovation, and then… drifted. Wandered. Lost the thread.</p>

            <div class="section-break">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

            <p class="no-indent">I had a dream recently. An old friend—a man from Kerala who became a US citizen, who successfully bridged two worlds—gave me his shoes. They were casual sports shoes, very comfortable. Green toe. Red collar. I felt grateful.</p>

            <p>The shoes were for walking, not standing still. Green for growth. Red for passion. Comfortable because they fit my natural strengths. The dream was telling me something: I am ready. The path suits me. I simply need to take the first step.</p>

            <p>But then comes the question: step where? Toward what? With what certainty?</p>

            <div class="pullquote">
                "I am not seeking certainty about the future. I am seeking clarity about who I am in this moment."
            </pullquote>

            <p class="no-indent">Today, I had a conversation that became a mirror. I sought perspective on my dream and ended up confronting every assumption, every fear, every rationalization I have been carrying. The conversation was uncomfortable because it was honest. It pushed me. It challenged me. It made me admit things I had not fully articulated to myself.</p>

            <p>And somewhere in that discomfort, something shifted.</p>

            <p>I realized I have been asking the wrong question. I have been asking: "Should I sell or should I hold?" But that is not the real question. The real question is: "What regret can I live with, and what regret will haunt me?"</p>

            <p>I can live with the regret of trying and failing. I have failed before. I have adapted before. Failure does not scare me.</p>

            <p>But the regret of not even trying? That sits heavier. That would follow me to my grave. That would be the voice in my final moments asking: "Why didn't you at least try? Why did you play it safe? Why did you choose preservation over creation when you knew—you KNEW—that preservation without transformation is just slow death?"</p>

            <div class="section-break">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

            <p class="no-indent">I am a householder, not a renunciate. I have a son. I have family. I have responsibilities. My spiritual practice does not give me permission to abdicate worldly engagement—it demands that I engage skillfully, without attachment to outcomes but with full commitment to action.</p>

            <p>The Buddha taught about right livelihood. About engaged presence. About the middle way between asceticism and indulgence. I have spent years studying non-dual awareness, consciousness theories, contemplative practices. I have explored what it means to "die daily." But all of that exploration was preparation for this moment: the moment when I must choose action from clarity rather than inaction from fear.</p>

            <p>I want my son to inherit land that is sustainable, profitable, and meaningful. Not just a piece of property that slowly erodes generation by generation, but a regenerative asset that produces food, income, and connection to something larger than market value. I want him to have what I am trying to create: a foundation outside the collapsing systems, a base of operations for weathering the future challenges I sense coming.</p>

            <p>But I also recognize the trap: am I attached to this outcome? Am I measuring my worth by whether I succeed at transforming these nine acres? Am I confusing service with ego?</p>

            <p>Perhaps. Probably. Almost certainly.</p>

            <p>And yet, what is the alternative? To do nothing because I might be attached? To avoid action because my motives might be impure? That is spiritual bypass masquerading as wisdom. That is fear dressed up in philosophical clothing.</p>

            <div class="pullquote">
                "I commit fully to the experiment and release the outcome. This is the householder's version of non-attachment."
            </pullquote>

            <p class="no-indent">I do not know if I will succeed. I do not know if mushroom farming will work in Kurukshetra. I do not know if vertical crops will be viable. I do not know if I can maintain focus long enough to see the experiments through. I do not know if the banking system will protect or devour my capital. I do not know if my son will value what I am trying to create. I do not know if I am acting from clarity or just sophisticated rationalization.</p>

            <p>What I do know is this: I am fifty-six years old. I practice dying every day. And when I ask myself—truly ask myself—whether today would be a good day to die, the answer is no. Not yet. Not because I fear death, but because I have not yet tried what I came here to try.</p>

            <p>The comfortable green shoes are waiting. The path of transformation is visible. The capital to fund the experiments is available. The vision for sustainable agriculture is clear. The desire to break the three-hundred-to-fourteen-to-zero pattern is strong.</p>

            <p>What remains is the hardest part: beginning.</p>

            <p>Not beginning with certainty. Not beginning with guarantees. Not beginning with perfect conditions or elimination of all risks or confidence that my distraction element will not sabotage me. But beginning nonetheless. Beginning with commitment to the process and release of the outcome. Beginning with eyes open to my own limitations and willingness to seek structure, accountability, and partnership to compensate for them. Beginning with the understanding that this is not about me proving something or achieving something or securing something—it is about offering what I have to give while I am still here to give it.</p>

            <div class="section-break">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

            <p class="no-indent">Someone reading this might think I have made my decision. But I have not. What I have gained is not certainty about the path forward, but clarity about what matters. And what matters is this:</p>

            <p>I would rather live with the consequences of committed action than die with the weight of never having tried.</p>

            <p>I would rather fail at transformation than succeed at preservation of something that is already dying.</p>

            <p>I would rather face my distraction element directly and build structures to work with it than use it as an excuse for inaction.</p>

            <p>I would rather leave my son nine acres of experimental sustainable agriculture—even if some experiments fail—than fourteen acres of unsustainable paddy-wheat contractor relationships.</p>

            <p>I would rather engage this moment fully, with all its uncertainty and risk, than stand on the sidelines waiting for perfect conditions that will never arrive.</p>

            <p>This is not bravery. This is not wisdom. This is simply honesty about what I can live with and what I cannot.</p>

            <p>The weight of not trying sits heavier than any possible failure.</p>

            <p>And so I walk forward, wearing comfortable shoes with green toes and red collars, into a future I am creating rather than waiting for.</p>

            <p class="no-indent">One step. Then another. Then we will see.</p>

            <div class="signature">
                — Roger<br>
                Week 46, November 2025<br>
                Kurukshetra, Haryana<br>
                49 days until year end
